The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (!timing_np) {
    pr_err("%pOF: could not find node '%s'\n", np, name);
}
```
Reason:
The unconditional error message was printed when the timing node was not found, even in cases where a fallback mechanism was available. This created unnecessary error logging and could mislead developers into thinking there was a critical issue when the absence of a timing node was acceptable.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (!timing_np)
    return -ENOENT;
```
Reason:
By removing the unconditional error message and replacing it with a return statement that indicates the absence of the timing node as a non-error condition, the code now allows for scenarios where a fallback is acceptable, thus preventing misleading error reports.

